X86_64 PublishMovdqa
(* A two-word record published with one 16-byte `movdqa` store and read
   with one 16-byte `movdqa` load: never torn.

   `rec` is declared `__int128`, a 16-byte location. P0 publishes version
   1, `{1, 1}`, then version 2, `{2, 2}` (low word, high word), each with a
   single `movdqa` from `xmm0`/`xmm1`; P1 reads the record once into
   `xmm2`. The SDM (Vol. 3A, "Guaranteed Atomic Operations") guarantees
   that on processors with AVX an aligned 16-byte `movdqa` access is single
   copy atomic, so each store is one 16-byte write and the load reads
   exactly one of them (or the initial record): `xmm2` is 0, version 1 or
   version 2, never a mix of the halves of two versions. A 16-byte value
   prints as one unsigned number, low word in the low 64 bits:
   `{1, 1}` = 0x10000000000000001, `{2, 2}` = 0x20000000000000002.

   herd7 has no `movdqa` (its x86 catalogue has no vector tests), so the
   verdict is argued here rather than checked against herd.

   Proved for every layout of the runner's image, the code verbatim, at
   declarations agreeing with the runner's on every cell:
   `VecPublish.x64_publish_movdqa_verbatim`, fault freedom
   `VecPublish.x64_publish_movdqa_verbatim_faultFree`
   (Promising/SLR/EnvG/X64VecPublish.lean). Proved from the runner's exact
   configuration:
   `VecPublish.Concrete.x64_publish_movdqa_verbatim_concrete`,
   `..._concrete_faultFree` (X64VecPublishConcrete.lean).
# flags: -fuel 300
# expect: Never
*)
{
__int128 rec;
0:xmm0=0x10000000000000001; 0:xmm1=0x20000000000000002;
}
threads { P0: writer done; P1: reader done; }
code {
writer:
  movdqa %xmm0,(rec)
  movdqa %xmm1,(rec)
  jmp done
reader:
  movdqa (rec),%xmm2
  jmp done
done:
}
exists (not (1:xmm2=0 \/ 1:xmm2=0x10000000000000001 \/ 1:xmm2=0x20000000000000002))
